---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import gatekeeperBlockedScreenshot from "../images/screenshots/gatekeeper-blocked.png";
import openAnywayScreenshot from "../images/screenshots/open-anyway.png";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const repositoryUrl = "https://github.com/jeremym/clipy-secure";
const releasesUrl = `${repositoryUrl}/releases/latest`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Install — Clipy Secure"
  description="Install Clipy Secure via Homebrew, a direct zip download, or by building from source, and verify the download yourself."
  structuredData={false}
>
  <section class="page-intro" aria-labelledby="install-title">
    <h1 id="install-title">Install Clipy Secure</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Clipy Secure is signed with the project's own certificate rather than an Apple Developer ID, and is not notarized.
      Pick whichever path you trust. It needs macOS 14 Sonoma or later, and asks for Accessibility permission on first
      run so it can paste for you.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section prose" id="homebrew" aria-labelledby="homebrew-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="homebrew-title">homebrew — recommended</h2>
      <hr />
    </div>
    <pre class="command-block"><code>brew install --cask jeremym/tap/clipy-secure
open -a ClipySecure</code></pre>
    <p>
      The fully-qualified cask name trusts the tap automatically — there is no separate <code>brew tap</code> step. The
      cask removes the quarantine flag on install, so the app launches without a Gatekeeper prompt. Updates come through
      <code>brew upgrade</code>; there is no in-app updater, and the Accessibility permission is preserved across
      upgrades because the signing identity does not change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section prose" id="zip" aria-labelledby="zip-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="zip-title">direct download</h2>
      <hr />
    </div>
    <p>
      If you would rather not use Homebrew, download the zip from the
      <a href={releasesUrl}>latest release <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>. Because the app is not notarized,
      macOS quarantines a fresh download and Gatekeeper blocks the first launch. You clear that once, either through
      System Settings or with a single Terminal command.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Download <code>ClipySecure-&lt;version&gt;.zip</code>, unzip it, and move <code>ClipySecure.app</code> to <code>/Applications</code>.</li>
      <li>Double-click the app. macOS refuses to open it, because it was not verified by Apple.</li>
      <li>Open <strong>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security</strong>, scroll to the <em>"ClipySecure" was blocked</em> notice, and click <strong>Open Anyway</strong>. Authenticate, then confirm <strong>Open</strong> in the dialog that follows.</li>
    </ol>

    <figure class="screenshot-document screenshot-document--secondary">
      <Image
        class="screenshot-image"
        src={gatekeeperBlockedScreenshot}
        alt="macOS Gatekeeper dialog reporting that ClipySecure could not be verified and was not opened"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>figure / first launch is blocked / <span aria-hidden="true">capture pending</span></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="screenshot-document screenshot-document--secondary">
      <Image
        class="screenshot-image"
        src={openAnywayScreenshot}
        alt="System Settings Privacy and Security pane with the Open Anyway button for ClipySecure"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>figure / System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Open Anyway / <span aria-hidden="true">capture pending</span></figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3 class="subhead">Terminal alternative</h3>
    <p>Remove the quarantine flag directly, with no trip through System Settings:</p>
    <pre class="command-block"><code>xattr -d com.apple.quarantine /Applications/ClipySecure.app</code></pre>
    <p>Either way, you only do this once per downloaded copy.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section prose" id="source" aria-labelledby="source-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="source-title">build from source</h2>
      <hr />
    </div>
    <p>The "trust nothing" path. Needs Xcode 16 or later.</p>
    <pre class="command-block"><code>git clone https://github.com/jeremym/clipy-secure.git
cd clipy-secure
./scripts/create-signing-cert.sh   # once per machine
./scripts/install-local.sh         # build, sign, install, launch</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>create-signing-cert.sh</code> generates a self-signed certificate in your login keychain — no Apple account
      needed. A certificate, even a self-signed one, matters here: a plain ad-hoc build pins the Accessibility grant to
      the binary's hash, so every rebuild looks like a new app and auto-paste breaks. Signing with a certificate makes
      the grant survive rebuilds. To update later, pull and run <code>install-local.sh</code> again.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section prose" id="verify" aria-labelledby="verify-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="verify-title">verify a download</h2>
      <hr />
    </div>
    <p>Every release ships the zip alongside a <code>.sha256</code> file. Confirm the bytes you downloaded:</p>
    <pre class="command-block"><code>shasum -a 256 ClipySecure-1.0.0.zip
# compare against the published .sha256</code></pre>
    <p>Then confirm the signature on the installed app. The designated requirement is identity-based and stable across releases:</p>
    <pre class="command-block"><code>codesign -d -r- /Applications/ClipySecure.app
# designated =&gt; identifier "com.clipysecure.app" and certificate leaf = H"a3ed032a6666d06c91d1c35147ad0e44093c1b5e"</code></pre>
    <p>
      That leaf hash is the project's certificate. If the output pins a <code>cdhash</code> instead of a certificate
      leaf, you have an ad-hoc build rather than a release. This is the honest substitute for Apple notarization:
      integrity rests on the published hash and the certificate you can check yourself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="document-section prose" id="uninstall" aria-labelledby="uninstall-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2 id="uninstall-title">uninstall</h2>
      <hr />
    </div>
    <p>Homebrew removes the app and quits it:</p>
    <pre class="command-block"><code>brew uninstall --cask clipy-secure</code></pre>
    <p>To also delete the app's stored data, add <code>--zap</code>, which trashes:</p>
    <pre class="command-block"><code>brew uninstall --cask --zap clipy-secure
# ~/Library/Application Support/com.clipysecure.app
# ~/Library/Preferences/com.clipysecure.app.plist</code></pre>
    <p>
      The encryption key stays in your login Keychain after uninstall. If you want it gone, delete the
      <code>ClipySecure</code> item in Keychain Access. Installed from source or a zip instead? Drag
      <code>ClipySecure.app</code> to the Trash and remove the same two paths by hand.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .subhead {
    margin: 1.6rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
</style>
